bruce wayne tons the cape and cow to  protect his beloved gotham city nearly a  century ago since then he has become one  of the most popular superheroes the  world has ever seen but that doesn't  mean there weren't a few hiccups along  the way for example while the caped  crusader is now famous for his no  killing rule older versions of the  character were rather less strict indeed  the early batman terrorized tortured and  killed his enemies proving a stark  contrast to the hero he later became  1940s batman issue 1 features one of  batman's most brutal and shocking kills  one of the issue's stories revolves  around the villainous doctor hugo  strange who uses a serum to transform a  handful of asylum inmates into mindless  destructive giants as they set out to  wreak havoc on the streets of gotham  batman tricks two of the giants into  beating each other to death he then  tracks down a second giant and hangs him  from a noose dangling from his batwing  before using gas pellets to knock  another from the top of a skyscraper  incredibly this isn't even batman's most  brutal kill in those early days of  gotham city as arguably the most  shocking callus of all can be found in  the character's debut story batman first  appeared in 1939's detective comics  issue 27 offering readers their first  glimpse of the man they would come to  know as a dark knight the story opens  with commissioner gordon entertaining  his young friend socialite bruce wayne  in his home during their discussion  gordon reveals that recently he has  become puzzled by a master vigilante  known as the batman soon after gordon is  called to the murder of a chemical  manufacturer named lambert and he offers  to take his friend along for the ride  wayne accepts of course claiming to have  nothing better to do the police  originally suspect the victim's son who  says he pulled the knife from his  father's back when he found him and  witnessed a figure climb out the window  the younger lambert says his father's  last word was contract and tells the  police that his father had just entered  into a contract with three men alfred  striker paul rogers and stephen crane  while gordon goes to see crane bruce  wayne returns home to put on his bat  suit because he's the batman bet you  didn't see that coming holy masquerade  unfortunately crane is already dead  although batman arrives at the scene  just in time to find his killers he  knocks one out and then throws the other  off the top of a two-story building and  while callously dropping someone from 30  feet up is bad enough that's only the  second worst thing batman does to  someone in his debut story after tossing  his foe off the roof batman discovers  that paul rogers and alfred striker are  due to meet in stryker's laboratory  their striker attempts to kill rogers  but batman intervenes pausing for just  long enough to explain strikers  dastardly plan to take control of the  apex chemical corporation then when  stryker pulls a gun to shoot them both  batman punches him so hard that he  breaks through a metal railing falling  into a vat of acid when a surprise  rogers points out what's happening  batman callously comments that it is a  fitting end for his kind later when  gordon tells the story to his wealthy  friend bruce wayne wayne says it all  sounds like a lovely fairy tale after  that the dark knight would kill again  multiple times using fire physical  beatings and even guns to dispatch his  foes  in fact the first few batman stories in  the world of detective comics read more  like a full-blown rampage than a career  in vigilantism as time went by however  dc comics decided to pull batman away  from his pulp fiction roots and make him  more of a genuine superhero and that  meant no killing by batman issue 4 he  was telling robin never to kill with  weapons of any kind and before long the  no kill rule had become one of the core  tenets of bruce wayne's crime-fighting  philosophy  check out one of our newest